Most fantasy stories are centered around some kind of artifact that
gives the holder tremendous power, but usually at some cost.  The
Amulet of Yendor in nethack is similar to this (limits your
capabilities), and so are named weapons.

But how about a new set of objects, called artifacts?  These would be
like named tools.  They should be very powerful, perhaps the most
powerful objects in the dungeon, but with strict limitations on their
use.  They could only be used once in a very great while, say
5000-10000 turns.  If used only at those intervals, they will always
behave correctly.  The cost could vary with the artifacts, but may
include:  temporary loss of stats (e.g. strength reduced by 50% for
100 turns), loss of hps, enormous food use, etc.

If used before the alotted time has passed, the effects would vary
depending on how close to the ``safe'' time it is, on the players
luck, on his standing with his god, etc.  Bad effects could include
destroy all armor/weapons, luck goes to -10, god gets angered, create
lots of monsters, permanent loss of stats, etc.

So what should these artifacts be?  Here's my suggestion:  Nikolai
Tolstoy, in the *Coming of the King* (the first book in a new
Arthurian trilogy) brings up the 13 Treasures of the Island of Prydein
(Britain).  These are all powerful artifacts named after the kings of
Britain.  He doesn't list all 13 in one place--the ones I've found so
far are:
   -Hamper of Gwydno:  Once a year, place some food in it.  It will be
    multiplied one-hundred-fold.  In nethack, it could be used to
    multiply corpses--stick a wraith corpse in it, get ten back.

   -Whetstone of Tudwal:  No description (yet).  Perhaps by applying
    it to weapons, their enchantment could be raised (also blessed and
    rustproofed).

   -Knife of Lawfroded:  No description.  Would have to somehow be
    better than current named weapons.

   -Sword of Riderch:  No description.

   -Harp of Teirtu:  No description.  Could possibly be used to
    charm/tame monsters.

   -Horn of Bran Galed:  Only a brief description, which refers to it
    as containing liquor (obviously not a musical instrument).
    Drinking from it would give effects of random potion(s).  For
    example, both extra healing and gain level if enough time has
    passed, or blindness and hallucination if too soon.  (Of course,
    unicorn horns let you get around this).

   -Dish of Rigennyd the Cleric:  Brief description which mentions it
    along with good food.

Someone brought up the idea of having oracles send players on quests.
Perhaps the quests could be to bring back named artifacts.  To make
sure they aren't found too early in the game, they could be scattered
randomly about the hell levels.  Bonus points should also be awarded
for escaping from the dungeon with them.

Well, enough rambling.  Anybody else like this idea?  If there's
enough interest, I'd be willing to do some more research on the
subject (I believe Tolstoy follows Celtic legends very accurately).

